---
/**
 * One route per recorded sample, and each one really runs.
 *
 * WHAT THESE PAGES ARE. `/playground/` itself is parked: the playground worth
 * having is an editor with iyi's own compiler running in the tab, and that
 * waits on language work. These routes are the part that honestly works today.
 * Each one carries one program, its recorded colouring from the compiler's own
 * lexer, and a run that fetches the module the compiler built from that file,
 * checks its digest against the record, and executes it in this page.
 *
 * So the lessons' deep links keep resolving to something that does something,
 * which is the whole reason the routes survived the parking of the page above
 * them. `index.astro` owns `/playground/` and a static route wins over a rest
 * parameter, so the bare path is not generated here.
 *
 * WHY A ROUTE PER SAMPLE RATHER THAN A PICKER THAT SWAPS IN THE BROWSER. Each
 * page carries exactly one program and one recorded colouring. The alternative
 * was one page holding all thirteen, which would have meant shipping every
 * listing to every visitor so a control could reveal one of them. A site
 * arguing that a program should link only what it uses does not get to do that,
 * and there is a second reason that matters more: a real URL resolves before
 * any script runs, so a lesson's link works with scripts off and survives being
 * bookmarked or shared.
 */
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import Playground from "../../components/Playground.astro";
import type { CuratedSample } from "../../playground/samples";
import { curatedSamples } from "../../playground/samples";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  /* One per recorded sample, and nothing else. The recording decides what
   * exists, so a sample that failed to record has no page rather than a page
   * that cannot run. */
  return curatedSamples.map((sample) => ({
    params: { sample: sample.id },
    props: { sample },
  }));
}

interface Props {
  sample: CuratedSample;
}

const { sample } = Astro.props as Props;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/*$/, "/");
---

<Page
  title={`Playground: ${sample.id} | iyi`}
  summary={`${sample.path}, cross-compiled to wasm32-wasi by the iyi compiler and executed on this page against a WASI host written in TypeScript. It does not compile, and it says so.`}
>
  <Playground sample={sample} />

  <section class="spread">
    <div class="to-wide honest">
      <hr class="rule" />
      <h2>What is not here</h2>
      <p>
        Three sentences, because two of these are structural and will not be
        fixed by finishing something.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing in this page compiles iyi.</strong> A browser has no
          linker, and producing a program out of a module shells out to a linker
          driver, which a page cannot do because it has no subprocesses. So the
          module this page runs was built elsewhere and editing the pane cannot
          change it, which the pane says the moment you type. A compile service
          would have closed that gap and was refused: compiling somewhere else
          proves only that somewhere else has a compiler.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>There are no threads, and there cannot be.</strong> GitHub
          Pages cannot set HTTP response headers, so{" "}
          <code>Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy</code> and{" "}
          <code>Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy</code> cannot be sent, so this
          document is never cross-origin isolated and{" "}
          <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> does not exist here. That rules out wasm
          threads and synchronous standard input through{" "}
          <code>Atomics.wait</code>. It is also why output arrives in the chunks
          the program wrote but only once <code>_start</code> returns: a wasm
          call in this page cannot be suspended.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>There is no standard input and no filesystem.</strong> Reads
          from descriptor zero succeed and report end of file, and no directory
          is preopened, so every path call fails the way it would under a host
          that granted no capabilities. One sample fails on purpose for exactly
          that reason and the recording marks it, because a target that cannot
          open a file is a thing worth knowing about the target.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <a href={`${base}playground/evidence/`}>The recordings behind this</a>{" "}
        say which bytes ran and what checked them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .honest {
    margin-block: 1rem 3rem;
  }

  .honest h2 {
    margin-block: 1.6rem 0.7rem;
  }

  .honest p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .honest ul {
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
    margin-block: 0 1.05em;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .honest li {
    margin-block: 0.65em;
  }
</style>
